1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,376
A 50 lakh home loan, and you pay the bank back 95,93,875 rupees?

2
00:00:06,376 --> 00:00:12,751
Yes, this is from Bank of Baroda's own calculator.

3
00:00:12,751 --> 00:00:14,490
That means almost double.

4
00:00:14,490 --> 00:00:17,573
You buy one house, but the bill is almost twice as much.

5
00:00:17,573 --> 00:00:20,655
And the biggest shock is still to come.

6
00:00:20,655 --> 00:00:26,380
Most of your EMI in the early years doesn't even go toward the house.

7
00:00:26,380 --> 00:00:31,332
This is Bhai's story. Steady job, already married,

8
00:00:31,332 --> 00:00:36,779
and one dream, a flat of his own. The bank uncle approved the loan,

9
00:00:36,779 --> 00:00:39,750
and sweets were handed out at home.

10
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:42,870
Papa put a hand on his shoulder and said,

11
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:45,210
just keep paying the EMI on time,

12
00:00:45,210 --> 00:00:48,720
everything else will take care of itself. Everyone believed it.

13
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:50,280
But is that really true?

14
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,017
Because a few years later,

15
00:00:52,017 --> 00:00:56,359
Bhai opened his loan statement carefully for the first time.

16
00:00:56,359 --> 00:01:00,267
And what he saw, he couldn't sleep that night.

17
00:01:00,267 --> 00:01:01,570
What was in it?

18
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:03,274
He had paid so many EMIs,

19
00:01:03,274 --> 00:01:07,108
and still the remaining debt was almost the same as before.

20
00:01:07,108 --> 00:01:10,942
So where did the money go? Straight into the bank's pocket,

21
00:01:10,942 --> 00:01:12,220
as interest.

22
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:14,348
So today, three questions. First,

23
00:01:14,348 --> 00:01:19,029
how does a 50 lakh loan become almost double? Second,

24
00:01:19,029 --> 00:01:23,710
in the early years, how much of the house is really yours?

25
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:24,632
And third,

26
00:01:24,632 --> 00:01:31,548
by paying just one extra EMI a year, how much interest and how many years do you save?

27
00:01:31,548 --> 00:01:34,775
Stay till the end for the third answer,

28
00:01:34,775 --> 00:01:37,080
it's the most useful one.

29
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:42,110
But first, let's go back to where this story began.

30
00:01:42,110 --> 00:01:43,940
At the bank counter,

31
00:01:43,940 --> 00:01:48,970
where Bhai happily signed every paper.

32
00:01:48,970 --> 00:01:51,525
The bank uncle opened the file. First thing,

33
00:01:51,525 --> 00:01:54,080
the bank doesn't pay the full price of the house.

34
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:58,460
You have to pay at least 20 percent from your own pocket.

35
00:01:58,460 --> 00:02:02,864
This is called margin, meaning your own share in the house.

36
00:02:02,864 --> 00:02:05,066
The rest is the bank's loan.

37
00:02:05,066 --> 00:02:09,030
And the real story begins with this loan.

38
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:13,345
Bhai's papers said, loan 50 lakh rupees.

39
00:02:13,345 --> 00:02:16,365
This original loan is called the principal.

40
00:02:16,365 --> 00:02:20,680
That is, the money the bank actually gave you.

41
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:24,539
The bank is not going to give this money for free.

42
00:02:24,539 --> 00:02:27,111
Borrowed money has a rent too.

43
00:02:27,111 --> 00:02:30,970
That rent is called byaaj, interest in English.

44
00:02:30,970 --> 00:02:34,430
Put these two together and you get the EMI.

45
00:02:34,430 --> 00:02:39,866
EMI means a fixed installment every month. The same amount every month,

46
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:43,820
for 20 years, without missing a single month.

47
00:02:43,820 --> 00:02:50,205
Now put a 50 lakh loan into the Bank of Baroda calculator.

48
00:02:50,205 --> 00:02:53,397
The interest alone comes to 45,93,875 rupees.

49
00:02:53,397 --> 00:02:56,590
That is almost as much as the original loan.

50
00:02:56,590 --> 00:03:00,726
Just think. You borrowed 50 lakh,

51
00:03:00,726 --> 00:03:07,815
and on top of that you paid the bank almost the same amount as rent.

52
00:03:07,815 --> 00:03:11,360
The total bill comes to 95,93,875 rupees.

53
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:16,370
But why does this happen? Interest isn't charged just once.

54
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:19,102
Every month, on the whole amount of loan that is left,

55
00:03:19,102 --> 00:03:22,290
new interest gets added.

56
00:03:22,290 --> 00:03:24,781
Think of it like a rented house.

57
00:03:24,781 --> 00:03:29,348
As long as you live in it, you pay rent every month.

58
00:03:29,348 --> 00:03:33,500
A loan is the same: the longer you keep it, the more rent you pay.

59
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,126
And 20 years is a very long time.

60
00:03:36,126 --> 00:03:39,503
For all those years the bank's money stays with you.

61
00:03:39,503 --> 00:03:43,630
That's why the rent also becomes so big, almost the same as the loan.

62
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:47,340
So is all this interest the bank's profit? No.

63
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:51,050
The bank also has to pay interest to its FD depositors,

64
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:54,760
and it has to cover the cost of staff and branches too.

65
00:03:54,760 --> 00:03:58,111
But for your pocket, the math is simple.

66
00:03:58,111 --> 00:04:02,718
The loan for the house is one thing, and on top of that almost the same amount again as interest.

67
00:04:02,718 --> 00:04:05,650
This is the real bill of your house.

68
00:04:05,650 --> 00:04:09,337
When my sister saw this number, she said, okay,

69
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:10,565
the interest is high.

70
00:04:10,565 --> 00:04:16,300
But with every EMI, the house slowly becomes a little more mine, doesn't it?

71
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,590
This is the biggest misunderstanding.

72
00:04:18,590 --> 00:04:23,628
In the early years, the house becomes yours very slowly.

73
00:04:23,628 --> 00:04:27,750
How slowly? But the real question starts now.

74
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:28,810
The second question.

75
00:04:28,810 --> 00:04:34,640
In the early years, how much of your EMI went into the house,

76
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:37,820
and how much into the bank's pocket?

77
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:41,000
The answer is hidden in a table.

78
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,968
This table is called an amortisation schedule. Hard name, simple job.

79
00:04:45,968 --> 00:04:50,484
How much interest went in each EMI, how much of the loan was paid off,

80
00:04:50,484 --> 00:04:52,290
it's all written down.

81
00:04:52,290 --> 00:04:54,055
The bank gives you this table,

82
00:04:54,055 --> 00:04:56,526
often you can find it in the app too.

83
00:04:56,526 --> 00:04:59,349
But most people never even open it.

84
00:04:59,349 --> 00:05:01,820
Bhai never opened it either.

85
00:05:01,820 --> 00:05:07,331
Now look at a 20-year schedule from Bank of Baroda.

86
00:05:07,331 --> 00:05:12,843
In the first year, a full year of EMIs was paid, 2,28,000 rupees in total.

87
00:05:12,843 --> 00:05:16,350
Now guess, how much of that was interest?

88
00:05:16,350 --> 00:05:18,738
The answer is 1,82,261 rupees.

89
00:05:18,738 --> 00:05:25,304
That means most of the year's EMI money went straight into interest.

90
00:05:25,304 --> 00:05:30,080
Very little went toward the actual loan.

91
00:05:30,080 --> 00:05:33,550
That means even after paying EMI for a whole year,

92
00:05:33,550 --> 00:05:35,719
the loan is almost the same as before.

93
00:05:35,719 --> 00:05:40,490
Only a very small part of the house is truly yours.

94
00:05:40,490 --> 00:05:43,573
Why is that? In the beginning, the loan is at its biggest.

95
00:05:43,573 --> 00:05:46,657
So the interest on it is also the biggest.

96
00:05:46,657 --> 00:05:49,740
And that eats up most of the EMI.

97
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:53,157
As the loan goes down, the interest goes down too.

98
00:05:53,157 --> 00:05:57,333
Then more of the EMI starts going toward the actual loan.

99
00:05:57,333 --> 00:05:59,990
But that happens only after many years.

100
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:02,846
This is called front-loaded interest.

101
00:06:02,846 --> 00:06:09,033
It means the bank collects most of its rent in the early years.

102
00:06:09,033 --> 00:06:12,840
The EMIs in the last years mostly pay off the actual loan.

103
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:16,389
Now do you understand why Bhai couldn't sleep?

104
00:06:16,389 --> 00:06:19,494
Even after paying EMI for a few years,

105
00:06:19,494 --> 00:06:23,930
the remaining loan in the statement looked like a mountain.

106
00:06:23,930 --> 00:06:25,809
And there was one more danger.

107
00:06:25,809 --> 00:06:28,439
Bhai's loan was on a floating rate.

108
00:06:28,439 --> 00:06:33,700
Floating rate means the interest goes up and down with RBI's decisions.

109
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:37,423
When interest goes up, the bank often doesn't touch the EMI.

110
00:06:37,423 --> 00:06:42,543
It quietly adds more years to the loan. And more years means

111
00:06:42,543 --> 00:06:44,870
more months of interest.

112
00:06:44,870 --> 00:06:48,141
So just paying the EMI on time is not enough.

113
00:06:48,141 --> 00:06:50,685
Papa was only half right.

114
00:06:50,685 --> 00:06:54,320
But there is a simple way out of this trap.

115
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:57,164
Mummy put down the tea and asked,

116
00:06:57,164 --> 00:07:02,852
So will we just keep paying the bank's rent for 20 years?

117
00:07:02,852 --> 00:07:06,170
Behen smiled. No Mummy, there is a way.

118
00:07:06,170 --> 00:07:09,223
The third question, and the most useful one.

119
00:07:09,223 --> 00:07:13,801
If you pay just one extra EMI in a year,

120
00:07:13,801 --> 00:07:18,380
what will happen? You might be shocked to hear the answer.

121
00:07:18,380 --> 00:07:23,867
This is called prepayment. That means, before time, by your own choice,

122
00:07:23,867 --> 00:07:26,860
putting extra money into the loan.

123
00:07:26,860 --> 00:07:31,350
This money directly reduces the actual amount you owe.

124
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:35,480
And that is its magic. When the principal goes down,

125
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:40,068
from next month you are charged less interest on it too.

126
00:07:40,068 --> 00:07:43,280
Every month, until the last day of the loan.

127
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:48,254
The Economic Times published a calculation. A 50 lakh loan,

128
00:07:48,254 --> 00:07:50,968
a little longer than Bhai's loan.

129
00:07:50,968 --> 00:07:55,490
Just one extra EMI a year, and nothing else changed.

130
00:07:55,490 --> 00:08:00,895
According to the report, about 18.31 lakh rupees in interest was saved. Just think,

131
00:08:00,895 --> 00:08:06,300
only one extra installment a year, and savings of lakhs of rupees.

132
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:10,237
And the loan also ends more than 5 years early.

133
00:08:10,237 --> 00:08:12,861
That means no EMI for years.

134
00:08:12,861 --> 00:08:18,110
That money can be used for the children's education or for old age.

135
00:08:18,110 --> 00:08:19,859
Why such a big difference?

136
00:08:19,859 --> 00:08:23,794
Because the extra money goes in during the early years,

137
00:08:23,794 --> 00:08:29,040
when the debt is biggest and the interest is cutting the most.

138
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:32,160
The same front-loading that was against you until now

139
00:08:32,160 --> 00:08:36,060
now works for you. The sooner you prepay,

140
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:38,010
the more interest you save.

141
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:40,145
Bhai asked Behen,

142
00:08:40,145 --> 00:08:43,989
but where will one extra EMI come from? Behen said,

143
00:08:43,989 --> 00:08:45,270
Diwali bonus.

144
00:08:45,270 --> 00:08:49,540
Or set aside a small part of the EMI every month.

145
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:54,286
By the end of the year, you have an amount equal to one EMI.

146
00:08:54,286 --> 00:08:57,738
Put it straight into the bank, as prepayment.

147
00:08:57,738 --> 00:09:01,190
Just this little task, once a year.

148
00:09:01,190 --> 00:09:05,393
But Papa was worried. Won't the bank charge a penalty?

149
00:09:05,393 --> 00:09:09,214
If you return the money early, won't they lose interest?

150
00:09:09,214 --> 00:09:10,360
The question was right.

151
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:14,349
Here a clear RBI rule works in your favour.

152
00:09:14,349 --> 00:09:16,343
On a floating rate home loan,

153
00:09:16,343 --> 00:09:21,130
banks cannot charge a penalty for prepayment or for closing the loan early.

154
00:09:21,130 --> 00:09:23,995
That means if your loan is on a floating rate,

155
00:09:23,995 --> 00:09:28,906
the bank cannot ask for any penalty when you put in extra money.

156
00:09:28,906 --> 00:09:32,180
This is your right, not the bank's favour.

157
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:36,347
Keep one thing in mind. When you prepay, tell the bank in writing,

158
00:09:36,347 --> 00:09:39,378
keep the EMI the same, reduce the years of the loan.

159
00:09:39,378 --> 00:09:42,030
Most of the interest savings are in this.

160
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:47,053
That same week, Bhai went to the bank and paid his first extra EMI.

161
00:09:47,053 --> 00:09:52,077
For the first time, the debt actually looked smaller on the statement. But wait,

162
00:09:52,077 --> 00:09:54,360
there's one mistake still left.

163
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:58,306
Now, the mistake many people make. They think,

164
00:09:58,306 --> 00:10:00,817
I save tax on the home loan,

165
00:10:00,817 --> 00:10:02,970
so why hurry to pay it off?

166
00:10:02,970 --> 00:10:06,442
Yes, there is a tax benefit. Under Section 24(b),

167
00:10:06,442 --> 00:10:10,348
on the interest of the loan for the house you live in,

168
00:10:10,348 --> 00:10:13,820
you get a deduction of up to 2,00,000 rupees a year.

169
00:10:13,820 --> 00:10:17,533
Deduction means you don't pay tax on that much income.

170
00:10:17,533 --> 00:10:22,173
Repaying the principal also gets a benefit under Section 80C,

171
00:10:22,173 --> 00:10:26,350
up to 1,50,000 rupees, together with things like PPF.

172
00:10:26,350 --> 00:10:27,364
But note this:

173
00:10:27,364 --> 00:10:30,406
both these benefits are available only in the old tax system.

174
00:10:30,406 --> 00:10:34,800
In the new tax system, you don't get these deductions on the house you live in.

175
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:37,567
And the biggest point: what you save in tax

176
00:10:37,567 --> 00:10:39,720
is only a part of the interest.

177
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:41,258
The rest of the interest you paid to the bank

178
00:10:41,258 --> 00:10:43,410
still went out of your own pocket.

179
00:10:43,410 --> 00:10:48,078
So what's the simple thing for you to do? Open your loan statement.

180
00:10:48,078 --> 00:10:51,898
See how much of this year's EMI went to interest,

181
00:10:51,898 --> 00:10:54,020
and how much went to paying off the principal.

182
00:10:54,020 --> 00:10:57,771
Then check whether your loan is on a floating rate or fixed.

183
00:10:57,771 --> 00:11:01,182
If it's floating, there is no question of a prepayment penalty.

184
00:11:01,182 --> 00:11:03,910
If you're unsure, ask the bank in writing.

185
00:11:03,910 --> 00:11:04,293
Yes,

186
00:11:04,293 --> 00:11:08,892
before prepaying, do keep some money aside for emergencies.

187
00:11:08,892 --> 00:11:12,724
And also check your tax calculation with your CA,

188
00:11:12,724 --> 00:11:14,640
especially the old or new system.

189
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:16,722
This is not investment advice.

190
00:11:16,722 --> 00:11:19,637
Every household's needs are different.

191
00:11:19,637 --> 00:11:22,968
But one thing applies to every household:

192
00:11:22,968 --> 00:11:25,050
understand your EMI table.

193
00:11:25,050 --> 00:11:28,987
Remember my brother's story. A 50 lakh loan,

194
00:11:28,987 --> 00:11:32,923
and the bill nearly double. But now he knows,

195
00:11:32,923 --> 00:11:36,860
an EMI isn't just to be paid, it has to be understood too.

196
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:41,437
In the next video, another big question. Is it better to buy a house,

197
00:11:41,437 --> 00:11:43,933
or to live on rent and save money?

198
00:11:43,933 --> 00:11:46,430
The answer might change your thinking.

199
00:11:46,430 --> 00:11:51,013
And now, one simple question for you.

200
00:11:51,013 --> 00:11:57,889
Is your home loan on a floating rate or fixed?

201
00:11:57,889 --> 00:12:04,000
Write in the comments, floating or fixed. I'll read them.
